post.description:
Estimate the cost of running this workflow + compare to a reference
table's existing footprint. Output keys:

  candidate:   {sql_chars, plan_rows, plan_bytes, plan_operators}
  reference:   {fqdn, rows, bytes, partitions} (null if not provided)
  compute:     {est_credits_per_hour, recent_hourly_credits_avg}
  storage:     {ref_active_bytes, ref_time_travel_bytes, ref_failsafe_bytes}
  ai:          {recent_cortex_credits, recent_cortex_calls}
  query_history: {n_runs, avg_seconds, avg_bytes_scanned} for similar SQL
  verdict:     LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH risk indicator

Fan out widget queries in parallel and return one response with per-widget status.

Body shape:
    {
      "widgets": [{"widget_id": str, "config": ChartConfig dict}, ...]
    }

Response shape:
    {
      "widgets": [
        {"widget_id": str, "status": "ok"|"empty"|"config_mismatch"|"error",
         "data": [...], "error": str|None, "row_count": int}
      ],
      "summary": {"ok": int, "empty": int, "errored": int, "mismatched": int, "total": int}
    }

put.description:
Update all roles for a user (replaces existing role assignments).
Useful for the frontend to sync role assignments.

Body:
    {
        "roles": ["DATA_ENGINEER", "BI_ANALYST"]
    }

Response:
    {
        "message": "Updated roles for user 'USERNAME'",
        "username": "USERNAME",
        "roles": ["DATA_ENGINEER", "BI_ANALYST"]
    }

get.description:
SSE endpoint for real-time cache invalidation events.

Establishes a persistent connection and pushes invalidation
events to the client in real-time (<50ms latency).

Supports reconnection via Last-Event-ID header — if the client
disconnects and reconnects, it can resume from where it left off.

Usage (JavaScript):
    const eventSource = new EventSource('/api/cache/stream');

    eventSource.addEventListener('cache_invalidation', (e) => {
        const data = JSON.parse(e.data);
        data.cache_keys.forEach(key => queryClient.invalidateQueries([key]));
    });

    eventSource.addEventListener('connected', (e) => {
        console.log('Connected:', JSON.parse(e.data));
    });

Response format (SSE):
    event: connected
    id: 1
    retry: 3000
    data: {"type":"connected","client_id":"abc-123",...}

    event: cache_invalidation
    id: 2
    data: {"type":"cache_invalidation","cache_keys":["users"],...}

post.description:
Check whether data has changed for a list of tables.

Compares current row timestamps against the last-known timestamps
stored in Redis. Returns a map of table_fqn -> has_changed (bool).
Frontend uses this to avoid unnecessary re-renders on tab switch.

Request body: {"tables": ["DB.SCHEMA.TABLE1", "DB.SCHEMA.TABLE2"]}
Response: {"DB.SCHEMA.TABLE1": true, "DB.SCHEMA.TABLE2": false}

delete.description:
ORGADMIN drops an account.

`DROP ACCOUNT <name> GRACE_PERIOD_IN_DAYS = <n>` — Snowflake holds the
account in soft-deleted state for the grace period; an ORGADMIN can
`UNDROP ACCOUNT` during that window. After the grace period expires the
deletion is irreversible.

Requires:
  - caller's role to be `ORGADMIN`
  - confirmation guard satisfied: `confirm_token` equal to `account_name`
    verbatim **or** `confirm` set to `true` in the JSON body
  - `grace_period_days` between 3 and 90 (validated by the body model)

Request body (``DropAccountRequest``)::

    {
      "reason": "decommissioning sandbox tenant",   // required
      "confirm": true,                               // or confirm_token below
      "confirm_token": "MY_ACCOUNT_NAME",            // optional, must == account_name
      "grace_period_days": 7                          // optional, 3..90, default 7
    }

── Required FRONTEND change (FE repo on hold — do NOT edit here) ──
File: apps/data360/src/app/services/org-accounts/hooks.ts
The FE must call this route with a JSON **body** instead of query params.
Add / update the drop function to::

    // BASE_URL = '/org-accounts'
    export async function dropOrgAccount(
      accountName: string,
      opts: { reason: string; confirm?: boolean; confirmToken?: string; gracePeriodDays?: number },
    ): Promise<DropAccountResponse> {
      const { data } = await apiClient.delete<DropAccountResponse>(
        `${BASE_URL}/accounts/${encodeURIComponent(accountName)}`,
        {
          data: {                                  // axios: DELETE body goes under `data`
            reason: opts.reason,
            confirm: opts.confirm ?? false,
            confirm_token: opts.confirmToken ?? null,
            grace_period_days: opts.gracePeriodDays ?? 7,
          },
        },
      );
      return data;
    }

The previous query-string contract (`?confirm_token=...&grace_period_days=...`)
is removed; sending those as query params will be ignored and the call will
400 unless the body's confirmation guard is satisfied.
